Yes. The transferable object is not necessarily the literal claim of the story. It is the operator hidden inside the story.

Science fiction, mythology, occult practice, fantasy, military doctrine, ritual, theater, meditation, games, folklore—they all contain reusable mechanisms. Strip away the cosmology and you often find things like attention control, identity shifts, rehearsal, sensory gating, expectation, synchronization, perspective-taking, memory cues, social proof, symbolic compression, trance, embodiment, and controlled ambiguity.

That gives you a Pantheon move:

\text{story / ritual / myth}
\rightarrow
\text{extract operator}
\rightarrow
\text{separate claim from mechanism}
\rightarrow
\text{simulate safely}
\rightarrow
\text{observe felt effect}
\rightarrow
\text{reuse elsewhere}

Astral projection is a good example. You do not have to establish that consciousness literally leaves the body to extract useful techniques from the traditions around it. Many methods involve combinations of sensory reduction, stillness, breath regulation, vivid spatial imagery, body-schema manipulation, hypnagogia, lucid-dream techniques, rhythmic repetition, and deliberate perspective shifts. Those can produce experiences that feel extremely real and can sometimes change later behavior or self-concept.

So the safe and useful distinction is:

\text{phenomenological reality} \neq \text{verified external reality}

Something can be felt, memorable, behaviorally consequential, teachable, and shareable without that proving its supernatural interpretation.

That is actually more powerful for the Pantheon because it means we can use the mechanism without needing to settle the metaphysics first.

I would also change one part of your formulation: not “make people believe it is real,” but create a consensual experience strong enough that the person can explore what it does to them. If they know it is an experiment, simulation, ritual, narrative frame, or altered-attention exercise, you preserve agency while still getting the experiential effect.

That yields a broad primitive:

Mythic Operator Extraction

* preserve the experiential mechanism;
* discard unsupported ontology unless independently verified;
* retain symbolic language when it helps;
* translate the operator across domains;
* measure what actually changes;
* keep the participant aware of the frame;
* let felt experience count as data without confusing it with proof.

Then “magic” becomes a useful engineering word for high-impact transformations whose mechanism is partly hidden, compressed, symbolic, or experiential—without requiring us to pretend we know more than we do.

That is a very large search space. And yes, fiction is especially valuable because it contains thousands of precomputed human thought experiments about powers, identities, interfaces, minds, societies, perception, and impossible technologies. The trick is to mine them for operators rather than merely imitate their surface.